<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llívia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iulia Libica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llívia is a Spanish town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> valley entirely surrounded by French territory, an exclave of Catalonia. As Iulia Libica it was the Roman centre of the Cerretani and the old capital of Cerdanya. It stayed Spanish after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659, which ceded only villages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, because it was held to be a town. Its pharmacy, dating from the 15th century, is among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llivia.jpg" alt="Llívia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bloguer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ll%C3%ADvia_des_del_castell.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
